    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0efcc71c_f69e97fb",
        "filename": "tests/graphite/ComputeTaskTest.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-24T13:33:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this really the buffer type we want here (and the input as well)? The design around these buffers types are that they are used for transfers from gpu to cpu. In other words on metal they are being blitted from gpu memory to the buffer and then mapped/read on the cpu. That is different than being used in a Compute call and the buffer just happening to also be mappable.\n\nMaybe we need to add a new BufferType to handle compute? In general do we expect that compute buffers need to be mappable on the CPU or is that just a bonus if they are? Maybe we need some sort of generic readBuffer call for compute? But for now I don\u0027t see that being a public use API so all support for it should be guarded by testing only compile flags.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 54,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 67
      },
      "revId": "e5274baf2dc99f936a677ee59c9fea0ff7b9cf37",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "172a2519_b3b1c7f5",
        "filename": "tests/graphite/ComputeTaskTest.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-24T18:06:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I imagine we\u0027ll have compute pipelines that operate on intermediate buffers that never have to be synchronized to the CPU (even on discrete GPUs) where  `MTLResourceStorageModePrivate`  might be preferred. This is sort of what I was trying to achieve with the `BufferType::kXferGpuToCpu` check but I probably misunderstood what that buffer type really means.\n\nPractically the only two use cases for CPU-readback on compute buffers that I can foresee are 1) unit tests like this one, and 2) if we exposed GPGPU compute functionality in the public API for users that don\u0027t care about graphics. I don\u0027t know if 2) is a use case that makes sense for Skia but maybe one day.\n\nWhat are your thoughts on what the layering would look like for a generic `readBuffer` call? Would that be a method on Context/Recorder? Would that require a task type of its own? It needs to end up in `MtlCommandBuffer` eventually since synchronization has to be done via the blit encoder.",
      "parentUuid": "0efcc71c_f69e97fb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 54,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 67
      },
      "revId": "e5274baf2dc99f936a677ee59c9fea0ff7b9cf37",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "abec79ca_84d51694",
        "filename": "tests/graphite/ComputeTaskTest.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 5065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-24T18:09:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "FYI, D3D12 can create a buffer on the DEFAULT heap, which is GPU-only; the UPLOAD heap, which is XferCpuToGpu; or the READBACK heap, which is XferGpuToCpu. It\u0027s possible we could emulate fully R/W mappable behavior with a pair of UPLOAD and READBACK buffers associated with a DEFAULT buffer, but that seems like it\u0027s hiding the cost from the client when this is supposed to be a low-level interface.",
      "parentUuid": "0efcc71c_f69e97fb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 54,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 67
      },
      "revId": "e5274baf2dc99f936a677ee59c9fea0ff7b9cf37",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
